{"mode": "replace", "new_string": "const INTERVIEW = [
  {q:"Do you know what you want?",a:"Yes. I want to lead creative operations at scale — building and running the engine that turns product into content across markets, efficiently, with technology.\n\nI spent 15 years at FARFETCH doing exactly that, rising from Production Manager to SVP. I ran a 600+ team across five countries, a €40M+ P&L, and 3.6M digital assets a year. I cut operational spend from 10% to 1% of sales using Lean, AI automation, and Virtual Model technology. That's the work I'm best at and the work I want to keep doing.\n\nWhat I don't want is a purely strategic role disconnected from the operational engine, or a creative-director role focused on aesthetics. My value is at the intersection — where creative quality meets operational discipline. I took a year to test the craft side with a furniture studio, and it confirmed what I already suspected: I love the craft, but my real edge is at scale.\n\nI'm open on industry — fashion is my native language, but content supply chains exist in D2C, marketplaces, tech, healthtech, and media. I'm based in Portugal and open to remote or Porto-area roles. I'm ready to start."},
  {q:"Why did you leave Farfetch / what have you been doing?",a:"After 15 years at FARFETCH I needed space to breathe and rebuild. I'd also carried a creative idea for a long time — furniture as collectible craft — and I wanted to test it with my own hands, not just manage others making things. So I built a workshop, designed a credenza for my own living room, and the feedback made me keep going. It became Rui Silva Studio.\n\nThe studio is a side venture, a labor of love I keep running when time allows. It's where I imagine, design, and build until something is done. It keeps my creative mind sharp. What it also did was confirm something I already suspected: my real edge is at scale. I love the craft, but the place I create the most value is leading the creative engine that makes craft possible at industrial scale. That's what I'm looking to do next."},
  {q:"Tell me about yourself",a:"I started in textile and graphic design, then joined FARFETCH early and spent 15 years there, rising from Production Manager to SVP of Creative Operations. The thread through all of it has been making creative work happen at scale without losing quality — whether that's a 600-person team across five countries, a €40M+ P&L, or 3.6 million assets a year. The wins I'm proudest of are operational: cutting spend from 10% to 1% of sales, automating 80% of product categorization, bringing Virtual Model tech in to halve cost-per-item. I recently took time to run a small furniture studio to test craft at boutique scale; it sharpened my conviction that my real value is at the intersection of creative and operations. That's what I'm looking to do next."},
  {q:"What's a weakness / blind spot?",a:"My drive for quality and precision is my biggest strength, and I've learned to watch it. Earlier in my career I could over-control under pressure — I've gotten better at distinguishing where precision matters (luxury quality, SLAs) and where 'good and done' beats 'perfect and late.' 360° feedback also showed me I under-claim my work — others consistently rated me higher than I rated myself. I'm learning to claim achievements plainly."},
